<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n33633" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Pangaeus</span></b> , i, m., and
			<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Pangaea</span></b> ,
		ōrum, n., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Πάγγαιον ὄρος</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a mountain of Thrace</i>, <i>on
				the borders of Macedonia</i>, <i>near
			Philippi</i>, now <i>Pilaf Tepeh</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Mons Pangaeus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					4, 11, 18, § 40</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">flerunt Rhodopeiae arces Altaque Pangaea,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Verg.
					G. 4, 462</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Luc. 1, 679</span></i>.—Hence,
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Pangaeus</span></b> , a, um,
				adj., <i>Pangœan;</i> also
			for <i>Thracian</i> (late <b>poet.</b>): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nemora,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sil. 2,
					73</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">juga,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Val.
					Fl. 4, 631</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">arx,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id. 1,
					575</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
